          "modality": "text",
          "summary": "The interviewee, Fernando Alonso Lopez Velasco (goes by 'Fer'), participates in a structured English-speaking examination administered by examiner Mahnoush Shafi. Across roughly four and a half minutes of recorded speech, he addresses three topic clusters—accommodation, music, and travel—in response to paired questions (general preference followed by a follow-up detail).\n\nContent-wise, he states a preference for houses over flats (citing dislike of neighbors, desire for spaciousness, and a private bathroom), notes he has lived in his grandparents' large house for about five years, and hedges his long-term plans with a conditional tied to finances. On music, he declares a strong fondness, offers the metaphor that music 'cures your soul,' estimates two to three hours of daily listening, rejects live-performance settings because of audience noise and encores, but carves out an exception for artists he already likes. On travel, he expresses enthusiasm for novelty (new places, people, relationships), admits he does not travel as much as he wishes, likes flying but dislikes turbulence, and names Italy and Japan as desired destinations, elaborating only on Japan's cuisine.\n\nIn terms of communication style visible in the transcript, several patterns recur: (1) frequent use of 'like' as a hedge, approximation marker, or filler ('like for five years,' 'like a big yard,' 'around like two, three hours,' 'when it's like a band'); (2) discourse-level openers that signal candidness ('To be honest,' 'Honestly I don't'); (3) self-repairs and restarts ('prefer to living in a house… than a flat'; 'I had thought about that until now,' which appears to be a negation slip for 'I hadn't thought'); (4) conditional and hedging structures that soften commitments ('I'm not really sure about it,' 'unless I have enough money,' 'at some point'); (5) lists delivered with escalating rhythm ('knowing new places, meeting new people, making new relationships'); and (6) occasional truncation where a qualifying clause is abandoned mid-thought ('but I try not to work with…'). The overall register is informal and conversational, with the interviewee shifting between blunt declarations and tentative elaborations, often within the same turn. No numeric measurements of voice or movement are inferable from the text alone."
        }
